<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#article-58">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Article 58</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Article 58 of the Penal Code of the Russian SFSR, in force from 25 February 1927, was the law under which people suspected of counter-revolutionary activity were prosecuted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Its sections punished treason, espionage, wrecking, terrorism, anti-Soviet propaganda and agitation, and even the failure to report such acts, many of them by death with confiscation of property. Sentences ran up to 25 years and were often extended without trial, and those convicted were known as political prisoners, apart from common criminals. In effect it allowed the secret police to arrest anyone deemed suspicious; the code was rewritten after Khrushchev denounced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinism</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The first section defined a counter-revolutionary action as any act aimed at overthrowing, undermining or weakening the power of the workers' and peasants' Soviets and the governments of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and its republics, or the external security of the state and the main achievements of the proletarian revolution. Article 58-1 was updated with new sub-articles in force from 8 June 1934. The other Soviet republics had laws of the same kind: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> it was Article 54 of the penal code, in Belarus Article 63.</p><p>The sub-articles reached far beyond armed revolt. Contacts with foreigners for counter-revolutionary purposes, help to the international <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a> and urging a foreign power to war or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> were punished like an armed uprising. If a member of the armed forces fled abroad after treason, his relatives could be imprisoned for 5–10 years or exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> for 5 years, even if they had only lived with him. Section 14, added on 6 June 1927, made counter-revolutionary sabotage, the careless or wilful failure to carry out defined duties, punishable by at least a year's loss of freedom and in aggravated cases by shooting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Prisoners sentenced under Article 58 were often released without the right to settle within 100 km of large cities. Because one section made propaganda and agitation against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> a crime and another punished those who failed to report it, the law gave the secret police a free hand to imprison anyone it suspected and became a political weapon. It was also applied to people outside the Soviet Union: in the Soviet occupation zone of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, people suspected of opposing the Stalinist regime, for example through contacts with organisations in the Western zones, were interned as spies under it, and 66% of the inmates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> special camp at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> were held on this ground.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In The Gulag Archipelago of 1973 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleksandr-solzhenitsyn">Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn</a> mocked the breadth of the law, writing that there was no step, thought, action, or lack of action under the heavens which could not be punished by the heavy hand of Article 58. The code was largely rewritten after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a> denounced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinism</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Article 58” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its camps held ordinary criminals and political prisoners, many sentenced without trial by NKVD troikas; petty crimes and jokes about the government could bring a term, and <b>Article 58</b> punished “counterrevolutionary activities”.</blockquote><a href="/societies/soviet-union">Social Hierarchy of Stalin's Soviet Union</a></li></ul></section>
